# CPU inference settings for llama-server on the HP.
# The installer copies this file to /etc/homelab/llama-server.env once and never
# overwrites it. Edit that copy, then: sudo bash hp/llm/install-llama-server.sh install
#
# LLAMA_ARG_* names are llama.cpp's own environment variables (llama.cpp v0.7.0).
# HOMELAB_LLAMA_* names are read only by the installer and the systemd unit.

# --- Model (default profile: 28 GB of RAM, about 26 GiB visible to Ubuntu) ----
# gpt-oss-20b: 21B-parameter MoE with about 3.6B active per token, MXFP4, ~13 GB.
# Few active parameters matter most here: the E5-2620's DDR3 memory bandwidth,
# not its core count, limits generation speed.
# Download (not done by the installer):
#   repo ggml-org/gpt-oss-20b-GGUF, file gpt-oss-20b-mxfp4.gguf
# Put it at the path below, for example by copying it to M:\gpt-oss-20b\ from Windows.
LLAMA_ARG_MODEL=/srv/models/gpt-oss-20b/gpt-oss-20b-mxfp4.gguf
LLAMA_ARG_ALIAS=gpt-oss-20b
LLAMA_ARG_CTX_SIZE=32768

# --- Network -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Listens on every interface (LAN and tailscale0). Every request except /health
# needs the API key in /etc/homelab/llama-server/api-keys.
LLAMA_ARG_HOST=0.0.0.0
LLAMA_ARG_PORT=10000
# Browser pages allowed to call the API. "localhost" lets pages served from a
# PC's own loopback, such as the ai-workspace dashboard on :9090, call it; they
# still need the API key. llama.cpp accepts one origin or this special value,
# not a list.
LLAMA_ARG_CORS_ORIGINS=localhost

# --- CPU ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# One Xeon E5-2620: 6 cores, 12 threads, AVX but no AVX2. Use the physical core
# count for generation; hyper-threads add little for a memory-bound workload.
LLAMA_ARG_THREADS=6
HOMELAB_LLAMA_THREADS_BATCH=6
# One NUMA node today, so no NUMA option. If a second CPU is fitted, uncomment
# this, then drop the page cache once before the first start:
#   sync; echo 3 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches
# LLAMA_ARG_NUMA=distribute

# --- Memory --------------------------------------------------------------------
# mmap the GGUF from the RAID array. Weights live in the page cache, so a restart
# reloads from RAM instead of disk, and the kernel can drop cold pages under
# pressure instead of killing the service.
LLAMA_ARG_LOAD_MODE=mmap
LLAMA_ARG_N_PARALLEL=1
# Prompt cache in RAM, MiB. llama.cpp's default is far larger than this box can spare.
LLAMA_ARG_CACHE_RAM=1024
LLAMA_ARG_FLASH_ATTN=auto
# systemd cgroup limits for the service. Above HIGH the kernel reclaims the
# service's pages (slower, no crash); MAX is the hard ceiling. 18G leaves room
# for Ubuntu, Samba and the always-on services stack (about 6 GiB).
HOMELAB_LLAMA_MEMORY_HIGH=16G
HOMELAB_LLAMA_MEMORY_MAX=18G

# --- API ------------------------------------------------------------------------
LLAMA_ARG_JINJA=1
LLAMA_ARG_ENDPOINT_METRICS=1
LLAMA_ARG_TIMEOUT=900
LLAMA_ARG_OFFLINE=1

# --- After the 96 GB upgrade ------------------------------------------------------
# gpt-oss-120b: 117B-parameter MoE with about 5.1B active per token, MXFP4, ~63 GB
# in three parts (repo ggml-org/gpt-oss-120b-GGUF). Point at the first part; the
# other two must sit next to it. Replace the matching lines above with:
# LLAMA_ARG_MODEL=/srv/models/gpt-oss-120b/gpt-oss-120b-mxfp4-00001-of-00003.gguf
# LLAMA_ARG_ALIAS=gpt-oss-120b
# LLAMA_ARG_CTX_SIZE=65536
# HOMELAB_LLAMA_MEMORY_HIGH=76G
# HOMELAB_LLAMA_MEMORY_MAX=80G
